The hook intercepts **every** `@earendil-works/*` request before Node's resolver and, when it resolves a file, does not validate that the resolved target is a loadable module. It also globally rewrites `Module._resolveFilename`, so it is easy to corrupt if `load()` runs more than once (e.g. reload/hot-restart in the extension host), causing an unbounded chain of wrappers. Guard against double-hooking so the original resolver is captured only once:
```js
function hookModuleResolution(piNodeModules) {
	if (Module._resolveFilename.__pillotHooked) return;
	const originalResolveFilename = Module._resolveFilename;
	const hooked = function (request, parent, isMain, options) { ... };
	hooked.__pillotHooked = true;
	Module._resolveFilename = hooked;
}
```

